findings.evidence:
The template writes, inside a literal <script> element:
      var preload_resource = {{ h.literal(resource_json) }};
      var resource_url = {{ h.literal(resource_url) }};
h.literal is a Markup subclass (ckan/lib/helpers.py:112), so it disables Jinja's autoescape (autoescape=True is set at ckan/lib/jinja_extensions.py:41). The values come from ckanext/textview/plugin.py:96-98: 'resource_json': json.dumps(data_dict['resource']) and 'resource_url': json.dumps(url). h.dump_json is a bare json.dumps wrapper (ckan/lib/helpers.py:1902-1903). json.dumps escapes the quote and the backslash; it does not escape '<' or '/'. The embedded dict is the resource dict, whose name, description, url, format and arbitrary __extras are user-supplied and only pass unicode_safe in ckan/logic/schema.py:29-58 - no HTML constraint. So a resource description containing the eight characters </script> terminates the script element early and everything after it is parsed as markup. The identical construct is in core at ckan/templates/dataviewer/base.html:8, var preload_resource = {{ h.literal(h.dump_json(resource)) }}.

The helper reads in full:
    @core_helper
    def csrf_input():
        return ''
It has no callers: grep for csrf_input across ckan/templates and ckanext/*/templates returns nothing, and a case-insensitive grep for csrf/xsrf across all .py, .ini and .html in ckan and ckanext returns only this function and the unrelated samesite handling in ckan/lib/repoze_plugins/auth_tkt.py. So there is no token generated, no token embedded in any of the state-changing forms, and no verification step. Authorization is ambient: identify_user (ckan/views/__init__.py:118) resolves the caller from the auth_tkt cookie via REMOTE_USER, and /api/action/<fn> accepts POST with application/x-www-form-urlencoded bodies (ckan/views/api.py:176-186), a content type that triggers no preflight. This is WEB-27's exact shape - a control whose name promises a decision and whose body has no path that says no - with the additional property that nothing calls it.

Lines 36-41 read:
    try:
        if not data['success']:
            raise CaptchaError()
    except IndexError:
        # Something weird with recaptcha response
        raise CaptchaError()
data is a dict from response.json(). A missing key in a dict raises KeyError, not IndexError, so the handler is unreachable for the case its own comment describes. The comment names the intent and the code does not implement it - the WEB-27 shape, read from the caller's side.
